# Docs: slim code fences

Static MDX code fences teach the **featured** component’s wiring. Incidental siblings (`Title`, `Text`, `Button`, labels, lorem) are placeholders — not product copy.

Live demos (`src/components/demos/**`) keep real consumer copy. Fences stay short.

## Do

- Self-close parts that are not the teaching point: `<Title />`, `<Text />`, `<Button />`, `<ButtonLink />`
- Keep structure, layout, and props that show how the featured part mounts (overlay `style`, `Stack` chrome, wrap shells)
- Keep props on placeholders only when they matter to the pattern (`variant="secondary"`, `href`, `radius="auto"`)
- Prefer one or two repeated shells over a full grid of copy-filled cards

## Do not

- Paste demo marketing copy, titles, or button labels into fences when the featured component does not need them
- Expand placeholder trees with `render`, `size`, `tone`, or style only so the fence “looks finished”
- Mirror the live demo line-for-line when half the lines are filler content

## Examples

| Avoid | Prefer |
| --- | --- |
| `<Title render={<h3 />} size="4">Studio Pro</Title>` | `<Title />` |
| `<Button>Open workspace</Button>` | `<Button />` |
| Four `FeatureCard` calls with full title/description props | Two `Glow` shells with `<Title /><Text />` inside |
| Full article paragraphs in a ProgressiveBlur fence | `{/* long prose + images */}` + `<Button />` for the CTA |

## Out of scope

- Live demo TSX — still real consumer product copy
- Ask-your-agent prompt fences — those stay plain-language consumer intents
- When the featured component **is** Title/Text/Button, show the props and children that teach that component
